  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
    "lesson_core_move": "Map each key time point t0 in $x(t)$ to t0/a in $x(at)$ by solving at=t0, then use that mapping to relocate starts, stops, corners, peaks, and zero crossings.",
    "section_count": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see $x(at)$, the feature you are tracking stays the same feature, but its time coordinate changes. The cue is the scale factor inside the time argument, and the amplitude stays unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(at)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To find the new location, solve $at=t_0$ for $t$. That gives $t=t_0/a$, so the time point at $t_0$ in $x(t)$ appears at $t_0/a$ in $x(at)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "at=t_0 \\Rightarrow t=\\frac{t_0}{a}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_0 \\mapsto \\frac{t_0}{a}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Base case: a peak at $t_0=6$ in $x(t)$ moves to $t=3$ in $x(2t)$. The peak is still a peak, and its amplitude does not change; only the horizontal placement changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Mapping One Key Time Point in $x(at)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "In $x(t)$, a peak occurs at $t_0=6$. Where does that peak appear in $x(2t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the scale factor $2$ inside $x(2t)$. Use the same rule from the tutorial: keep the peak, solve $at=t_0$, and find the new time coordinate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}2t&=6\\\\t&=3\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the peak appears at $t=3$ in $x(2t)$. The peak itself does not change, and its amplitude stays the same; only the horizontal position moves.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping Start and Stop Times",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "In $x(t)$, the signal is nonzero on $2 \\le t \\le 10$. Where is it nonzero in $x(2t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the nonzero support interval. Use the same rule from the tutorial: map each endpoint with $2t=t_0$ and then rewrite the new interval in order.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}2t&=2 &\\Rightarrow&\\ t=1\\\\2t&=10 &\\Rightarrow&\\ t=5\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is nonzero on $1 \\le t \\le 5$ in $x(2t)$. The start and stop times move independently, and because the scale factor is positive, the interval keeps the same order.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Mapping Corners and Peaks",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "In $x(t)$, a piecewise waveform has a corner at $t=1$, a peak at $t=4$, and another corner at $t=7$. Where do these three features appear in $x(3t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a waveform with several labeled features. Use the same rule on each one: solve $3t=t_0$, keep the label, and map every original time to $t_0/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}t_0=1&\\Rightarrow t=\\frac{1}{3}\\\\t_0=4&\\Rightarrow t=\\frac{4}{3}\\\\t_0=7&\\Rightarrow t=\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the corner at $t=1$ moves to $t=\\frac{1}{3}$, the peak at $t=4$ moves to $t=\\frac{4}{3}$, and the corner at $t=7$ moves to $t=\\frac{7}{3}$. Listed left to right, the features stay in the same order because all three times are divided by the same positive factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Mapping Zero Crossings",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "In $x(t)$, the signal crosses zero at $t=-6$, $t=0$, and $t=9$. Where are the zero crossings in $x(3t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a set of zero-crossing times, including a negative time and the origin. Use the same rule on each one: solve $3t=t_0$ and map every crossing time to $t_0/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}t_0=-6&\\Rightarrow t=-2\\\\t_0=0&\\Rightarrow t=0\\\\t_0=9&\\Rightarrow t=3\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the zero crossings in $x(3t)$ are at $t=-2$, $t=0$, and $t=3$. The origin stays fixed, and the order is preserved because every time is divided by the same positive factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "How Time Scaling Moves Key Time Points",
        "section_index": 6,
        "section_title": "Rebuilding a Scaled Time Axis from Key Points",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A signal in $x(t)$ has key points at $t=-4$ (zero crossing), $t=-1$ (corner), $t=2$ (peak), and $t=5$ (stop time). Rebuild the key-point axis for $x(2t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a mixed list of labeled key points. Use the same rule on each one: solve $2t=t_0$, keep the label attached, and then rebuild the axis from left to right.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}-4&\\mapsto -2 && \\text{zero crossing}\\\\-1&\\mapsto -\\frac12 && \\text{corner}\\\\2&\\mapsto 1 && \\text{peak}\\\\5&\\mapsto \\frac52 && \\text{stop time}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-2\\ (\\text{zero crossing}) < -\\frac12\\ (\\text{corner}) < 1\\ (\\text{peak}) < \\frac52\\ (\\text{stop time})",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the scaled time axis for $x(2t)$ is zero crossing at $t=-2$, corner at $t=-\\frac12$, peak at $t=1$, and stop time at $t=\\frac52$. The labels stay attached, and the left-to-right order is preserved.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
